**Theorem 6.4 (large actual initial fiber with a common post-update boundary).**
There exists one initial $q_H$ fiber $\mathcal F_s\subseteq\mathcal B_s$ with

$$
|\mathcal F_s|\ge K_s,
\qquad
K_s=\left\lceil
\frac{C_{4s-1}\binom{4s}{2s}}
 {\min\{(s+1)^2,22s\}}
\right\rceil.
\tag{DAF.18}
$$

Choose a largest such fiber using a fixed public ordering to break ties, before
choosing the hidden source. On every member, one actual guarded $\rho$ accepts,
installs $\rho t$ and gives the same current boundary $(0,E_1,6s)$. An immediately
following $\rho$ executes reject because its whole candidate has $10s>H$ leaves.

Proof. Theorem 6.2 and pigeonhole give a fiber of size at least
$T_s/(s+1)^2$. For the stronger concentration estimate let
$Z=k_0^2+k_1^2$. Lemma 6.3 and Markov's inequality give more than half the
balanced words with $Z\le10s$. By (DAF.13), these lie in
$P^2+Q^2\le5s$. Each axis of the step-two grid $I_s^2$ has at most
$\sqrt{5s}+1$ points in the enclosing square. Its total point count is at most

$$
(\sqrt{5s}+1)^2\le11s\quad(s\ge1),
$$

since $2\sqrt{5s}\le5s$ and $1\le s$. Thus some boundary fiber contains
at least $1/(22s)$ of all words. Every word has exactly $C_{4s-1}$ bracketings,
all in its same boundary fiber, so the same fraction holds for actual trees.
Taking the stronger bound and the integer ceiling proves (DAF.18).

The fiber choice depends only on public $s$, not the chosen hidden member.
Its computation, prior representation and installation are not free source
advice. TM30.1 gives the post-update tag-0 boundary: initial tag 1 accepts once,
with new $E=E_1$ and size $6s$, while its next candidate size is $10s$.
The attempted second update has an actual reject label and successor; it is
not an illegal unexecuted operation. Distinct initial trees remain distinct
after the accepted update, by the injectivity supplier recalled in Lemma 7.1.

